 """Decide which files in a project tree are excluded by its .packignore files.
 
-This currently delegates to `packignore`, the legacy exclusion engine (see
-docs/PACKIGNORE.md), via the client in tools/.
+This is a pure-Python reimplementation of `packignore`, the legacy exclusion
+engine. docs/PACKIGNORE.md describes the intent but is out of date; the rules
+below are what packignore actually does.
+
+Parsing (per line, after `str.splitlines()`):
+  - The line is stripped of surrounding whitespace (`str.strip()`); blank lines
+    are skipped. There are no comments and no escapes: `#` and `\\` are literal.
+  - A leading `!` negates the rule (only one is consumed).
+  - Trailing `/`s make the rule match directories only; leading `/`s anchor it.
+    A rule whose remaining text contains `/` is anchored too. Anchored rules
+    match the path relative to the directory holding the .packignore; others
+    match a single name at any depth below it.
+  - Braces are expanded: the leftmost `{...}` (up to the first `}`) is split on
+    `,`; each alternative is followed by the expansion of the rest. Text inside
+    an alternative is not expanded again.
+  - `*` matches any run of characters within a name and `?` exactly one. A whole
+    path segment `**` matches zero to three directory levels; any other `**` is
+    a plain `*`. `[` is literal.
+
+Precedence (for each file, consider it and each of its parent directories):
+  1. A match on a deeper path wins (the file itself beats its directories).
+  2. Then the rule from the deeper .packignore wins.
+  3. Then the more specific rule wins: the one with more characters other
+     than `*?{},/`.
+  4. Then the later rule in the file wins.
+The winning rule decides: excluded, or (if negated) included.
+
+A .packignore is only read if its directory is not itself excluded (the root
+one is always read). A rule never matches the directory holding its file.
 """
 
 import os
-import subprocess
